"Microsoft Word - CDENewsVol4NoS5_Text1.docx (https://www.constrained-evo.shigenobulab.org/docs/CDENewsVol4NoS5s.pdf)\nciteturn18view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn14view2\",\"lineno\":6}); Total lines: 141\nL0@P0: 新学術領域研究\nL1@P0: 進化の制約と方向性\nL2@P0: 〜微生物から多細胞生物までを貫く表現型進化原理の解明〜\nL3@P0: 号外 食虫植物ハエトリソウの記憶の仕組みを解明\nL4@P0-1: Constrained & Directional Evolution Newsletter Vol. 4 No. S5 (2020)表紙：米国ノースカロライナ州に自生するハエトリソウ Dionaea muscipula\nL5@P1-2: （基礎生物学研究所 須田啓、長谷部光泰）1\nL6@P2: Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized\nL7@P2: in transgenic Venus flytrap\nL8@P2: Hiraku Suda, Hiroaki Mano, Masatsugu Toyota, Kenji Fukushima,\nL9@P2: Tetsuro Mimura, Izuo Tsutsui, Rainer Hedrich, Yosuke Tamada,\nL10@P2: Mitsuyasu Hasebe\nL11@P2: Nature Plants (2020) 6, 1219-1224\nL12@P2: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41477-020-00773-1\nL13@P2: The leaves of the carnivorous plant Venus flytrap, Dionaea muscipula (Dionaea) close\nL14@P2: rapidly to capture insect prey. The closure response requires two successive mechanical\nL15@P2: stimuli to sensory hairs on the leaf blade within approximately 30 seconds. An unknown\nL16@P2: biological system in Dionaea is thought to memorize the first stimulus and transduce the\nL17@P2: signal from the sensory hair to the leaf blade. Here, we link signal memory to calcium\nL18@P2: dynamics using transgenic Dionaea expressing a Ca2+ sensor. Stimulation of a sensory hair\nL19@P2: caused an increase in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration ([Ca2+]cyt) starting in the sensory hair\nL20@P2: and spreading to the leaf blade. A second stimulus increased [Ca2+]cyt to an even higher\nL21@P2: level, meeting a threshold that is correlated to the leaf blade closure. Because [Ca2+]cyt\nL22@P2: gradually decreased after the first stimulus, the [Ca2+]cyt increase induced by the second\nL23@P2: stimulus was insufficient to meet the putative threshold for movement after about 30\nL24@P2: seconds. The Ca2+ wave triggered by mechanical stimulation moved an order of magnitude\nL25@P2: faster than that induced by wounding in petioles of Arabidopsis thaliana4 and Dionaea.\nL26@P2: The capacity for rapid movement has evolved repeatedly in flowering plants. This study\nL27@P2: opens a path to investigate the role of Ca2+ in plant movement mechanisms and their\nL28@P2-3: evolution.2\nL29@P3: 食虫植物ハエトリソウの記憶の仕組みを解明\nL30@P3: 2020 年 10 月 6 日 自然科学研究機構基礎生物学研究所 プレスリリース\nL31@P3: https://www.nibb.ac.jp/press/2020/10/06.html\nL32@P3: 食虫植物は葉で小動物を誘引、捕獲、消化、吸収し、栄養としています。ハエトリソウはアメリカ合衆国\nL33@P3: のノースカロライナ州とサウスカロライナ州だけに分布する食虫植物です。二つ折りになった葉の上に毛\nL34@P3: （感覚毛）が生えており（図１）、1 回触っただけでは閉じませんが、30 秒以内にもう 1 回触ると、約 0.3 秒\nL35@P3: で閉じ、小動物を挟み込んで食べてしまいます（図１）。このことは、最初の刺激を 30 秒間記憶している\nL36@P3: ことを示しています。脳も神経も無い植物が記憶することから、その仕組みは広く興味が持たれ、多くの\nL37@P3: 研究者が研究を行ってきました。そして、1988 年にドイツのホディックとシーバースは、カルシウムイオン\nL38@P3: 濃度変化が関与しているのではないかという仮説を提唱しました（図２）。しかし、ハエトリソウでカルシウ\nL39@P3: ムイオン濃度を細胞にダメージを与えずに測定する方法が無く、真偽が不明でした。基礎生物学研究\nL40@P3: 所の須田啓大学院生真野弘明特任助教、玉田洋介助教（現宇都宮大学）、長谷部光泰教授らを中心\nL41@P3: とした研究グループは、ハエトリソウへの遺伝子導入技術（外来遺伝子を生物に導入する技術）を確立\nL42@P3: し、カルシウムイオンと結合して緑色蛍光を発するタンパク質の遺伝子をハエトリソウに導入しました。そ\nL43@P3: して、細胞内カルシウムイオン濃度変化を可視化することに成功しました。その結果、1 回目の刺激で細\nL44@P3: 胞内カルシウムイオン濃度が上昇し、2 回目の刺激で 1 回目の細胞内カルシウムイオン濃度に上乗せ\nL45@P3: して、さらにカルシウムイオン濃度が上昇、閾値（限界値）を超えることで葉が閉じることを発見しました\nL46@P3: （図３）。一方、1 回目の刺激で上昇した細胞内カルシウムイオン濃度は、時間とともに減少し（図 3）、ホ\nL47@P3: ディックとシーバースの仮説のように（図 2）、約 30 秒を超えると、2 回目の刺激を与えても、細胞内カル\nL48@P3: シウムイオン濃度が閾値を超えないために、葉が閉じないことがわかりました。この観察結果から、ハエト\nL49@P3: リソウの記憶はカルシウムイオン濃度変化によって説明できることがわかりました。\nL50@P3: 今後は、接触刺激がどのような仕組みでカルシウムイオン濃度を変化させるか、変化したカルシウムイ\nL51@P3: オン濃度がどのように運動を引き起こすか、そして、ハエトリソウの速い運動が普通の植物の何を変える\nL52@P3: ことで進化できたのかを解明することが課題です。そして、将来的には、動かない普通の植物を動くよう\nL53@P3: にできるかもしれません。\nL54@P3: この成果は英国時間 2020 年 10 月 5 日付で Nature Plants 誌に掲載されます。\nL55@P3: cite研究の背景 L56@P3: 普通の植物は根から栄養を吸収します。しかし、湿地のような貧栄養地では、根から栄養を吸収でき\nL57@P3: ません。食虫植物は小動物を誘引、捕獲、消化、吸収することで、根だけではなく、葉から栄養を摂取し、\nL58@P3: 貧栄養地でも生育することができます。食虫植物の 1 種であるハエトリソウはアメリカ合衆国のノースカロ\nL59@P3: ライナ州とサウスカロライナ州の湿地帯だけに自生します。二つ折りになった葉の縁にたくさんの刺があ\nL60@P3: るとともに、葉の上面に 6 本の細い毛（感覚毛）が生えており（図 1）、条件が良いと約 0.3 秒で閉じます。\nL61@P3: 葉が閉じる仕組みは、曲がる側の細胞で細胞内のカリウムイオンと塩素イオンが細胞外に放出され、\nL62@P3: 細胞内の浸透圧が細胞外より減少し、水が細胞内から細胞外に出ることで、細胞の細胞膜に囲まれた\nL63@P3: 部分が収縮するのではないかと考えられています。野菜を塩漬けにすると萎れる状態です。これだけだ\nL64@P3: と、動きはゆっくりなのですが、ハエトリソウの葉は弾性があり、葉は外側に反り返った状態になっている\nL65@P3: ため（図 1）、座屈現象（構造物に徐々に荷重を加えると、あるところで急に大きく変形する現象）によっ\nL66@P3-4: て葉が一気に表側に反り返ることが知られています。下敷きがぺこぺこ動くのと同じ現象です。3\nL67@P4: 図１ ハエトリソウの自生（左）と捕虫（中、右）。葉の表側にある感覚毛（中）を 2 回刺激すると葉が閉じる（右）。閉\nL68@P4: じた葉（右）はコオロギを捕獲している。\nL69@P4: 閉じる速度に加え、ハエトリソウの葉の持つもう一つの巧妙な点は、感覚毛を 1 回触っただけでは閉じ\nL70@P4: ず、30 秒以内にもう 1 回触ると閉じる点です。2 回さわらないと閉じないことで、偶然、雨やゴミなどがあ\nL71@P4: たっても閉じないメリットがあると考えられています。2 回の刺激で閉じるということは、1 回目の刺激を記\nL72@P4: 憶する仕組みがあるはずです。また、約 30 秒を超えると 2 回目の刺激で閉じなくなるということは、記憶\nL73@P4: は約 30 秒しか保持されないことになります。これまでこのハエトリソウの短期記憶の仕組みについて多く\nL74@P4: の研究が行われてきました。感覚毛を刺激すると、動物の神経細胞のように、葉の電位変化（活動電位）\nL75@P4: が起こることが、1873 年に発表されました。その後、動物細胞で、一次情報として細胞膜上を伝わる活\nL76@P4: 動電位が、細胞膜にあるカルシウムチャネル（カルシウムイオンを選択的に通過させる通路）を制御し、\nL77@P4: 細胞に変化を起こすことがわかってきました。植物細胞でも、活動電位によってカルシウムチャネルが活\nL78@P4: 性化され、細胞外から細胞内へカルシウムイオンが流入し、原形質流動などの細胞の働きが変化するこ\nL79@P4: とがわかってきました。このような状況のもと、ドイツのボン大学のホディックとシーバースは、ハエトリソウ\nL80@P4: の活動電位が細胞内カルシウムイオン濃度を上昇させ、その結果、葉が運動するというモデルを提唱し\nL81@P4: ました。さらに、カルシウムイオン濃度変化によって記憶現象が説明できるのではないかという仮説を提\nL82@P4: 唱しました（図２）。この仮説は、1）感覚毛を刺激すると活動電位が発生し、活動電位によって細胞内の\nL83@P4: カルシウムイオン濃度が上昇する、2）カルシウムイオンあるいはそれによって制御される分子が閾値を\nL84@P4: 超えた量まで蓄積すると葉が閉じる、3）1 回の刺激ではカルシウムイオンの上昇は閾値を超えることが\nL85@P4: できず、閾値を超えるには、少なくとも 2 回の刺激が必要である、4）カルシウムイオン濃度は 1 回目の刺\nL86@P4: 激で上昇後、徐々に減少し、30 秒以上たつと、２回目の刺激を与えてもカルシウムイオン濃度が閾値を\nL87@P4: 超えることができないというものです。\nL88@P4: しかし、ホディックとシーバースの仮説を検証するには、細胞内のカルシウムイオン濃度がどのように変\nL89@P4: 化するかを観察することが必要です。しかし、ハエトリソウにおいて細胞内カルシウムイオン濃度を検出\nL90@P4: する方法が無く、提唱から 30 年以上に亘って、仮説の真偽がわかりませんでした。\nL91@P4: cite研究の成果 L92@P4: 細胞内のカルシウムイオン濃度を検出するには、カルシウムイオンと結合すると光を発するようなセン\nL93@P4: サータンパク質をハエトリソウで働かせることが必要です。そのためには、センサータンパク質の遺伝子\nL94@P4: をハエトリソウに導入することが不可欠です。須田啓氏は、中学校で食虫植物の自由研究を行い、食虫\nL95@P4: 植物において、獲物を捕らえたという信号が葉全体にどのように伝わるかに興味を持ちました。この興味\nL96@P4: を追求するため、大学院生として基礎生物学研究所で研究を始めました。ハエトリソウに遺伝子導入す\nL97@P4: る技術を開発するため、２年半に渡って、さまざまな実験を繰り返しましたが、失敗が続きました。しかし、\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA single touch can provide sufficient mechanical stimulation to trigger Venus flytrap closure | PLOS Biology (https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pbio.3000740)\nciteturn18view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn15view0\",\"lineno\":269}); Redirected to URL: https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740; Total lines: 671\nL255: The model reproduced the experimentally found bounds for double deflections and predicted that if the deflections are too fast and/or too small, more than two deflections might be required to trigger trap closure (red area in cite106†Fig 3A ) because a single deflection is not sufficient to elicit an AP (cite106†Fig 3B ), something we also observed in our experiments (cite87†Fig 1C , middle). Similarly, the model showed that very low angular velocities (ω< 0.04 rad s^{−1}) cannot fill up the RP.\nL256: Unexpectedly, the model predicted a range of intermediate angular velocities (0.04 rad s^{−1} <ω< 10 rad s^{−1}), at which a single deflection is sufficient to elicit the two or more APs that are necessary to initiate trap closure (cite106†Fig 3B ).\nL257: Download:\nL258: \nL259:   * cite107†PNG larger image L260:   * cite108†TIFF original image L261: \nL262: Fig 3. ECB model.\nL263: (A) Prediction of the number of action potentials triggered for a single deflection with consecutive waiting time as function of angular displacement (“disp.”) θ and velocity ω, confronted with experimental observations for cases in which trap closure was triggered by single (blue circles) and double deflections (green circles).\nL264: (B) Number of deflections necessary to trigger trap closure with phases of one, two, or three deflections compared to the findings for single- (blue circles) and double-deflection (green circles) experiments. For the underlying data, see sheet Figs 3A and B in cite66†S1 Data . ECB, electromechanical charge buildup.\nL265: cite109†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.g003†doi.org L266: ### A single deflection at intermediate angular velocity triggers trap closure\nL267: As single-deflection trap triggering is contrary to the accepted view, we wanted to experimentally test this prediction of the model. Indeed, we observed trap closure induced by single deflections at lower angular velocities (cite110†S2 Video ). To narrow down the range in which this occurs, we repeatedly deflected the same sensory hair with varying angular velocities until the trap closed. Between two consecutive deflections, we waited 2 min for the trap to recover and any RP to dissipate.\nL268: The lower boundary of the angular velocity ω required for single-deflection closure was determined by incrementally increasing it after every deflection (n = 17). An initial velocity below 0.009 rad s^{−1} was chosen, which never resulted in trap closure. Subsequent single deflections were performed at increasing velocities until the trap shut (cite111†Fig 4A ).\nL269: The higher boundary was similarly determined by starting with a velocity ω > 3 rad s^{−1}, followed by a stepwise decrease until the trap shut (n = 9).\nL270: Additionally, we performed another set of single-deflection experiments (n = 5), in which the speed of the force probe was kept constant, leading to an intermediate angular velocity between 0.2 and 0.4 rad s^{−1}, while the angular displacement θ was gradually increased during subsequent deflections in order to get the lower boundary of θ required to trigger trap closure by a single deflection.\nL271: Download:\nL272: \nL273:   * cite112†PNG larger image L274:   * cite113†TIFF original image L275: \nL276: Fig 4. Slow single deflections lead to trap closure.\nL277: (A) Successive single displacements (“disp.”) of the sensory hair with increasing angular velocity while the torque is recorded. (B) Individual single-deflection experiments showing the stimulus that initiated trap closure (green circles) and the preceding, nontriggering deflection (red triangles). The specific experiments shown in (A), (C), and (D) are indicated.\nL278: (C and D) Single deflections at an intermediate velocity that led to trap triggering where either (C) two APs were generated during bending (on the way in), or (D) one AP is fired at the beginning of bending (on the way in) and a second at the end of the return (on the way out). Only the data up to the start of the trap closure were used. The data affected by motion artifacts are indicated by dotted lines. For the underlying data, see sheets Fig 4A to Fig 4D in cite66†S1 Data . AP, action potential.\nL279: cite114†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.g004†doi.org L280: All the single deflections resulting in trap closure, together with the preceding stimuli for which no trap closure was observed, define the region where a single deflection triggers closure (cite111†Fig 4B and cite100†S1C Fig ). The model output for single-deflection triggers covers a similar parameter space as experimentally obtained for angular deflection θ versus angular velocity ω.\nL281: We observed that a single deflection can trigger trap closure at intermediate angular velocities of the deflection (0.03 rad s^{−1} ≤ ω ≤ 4 rad s^{−1}) but is not sufficient at slower or faster angular velocities (see cite115†S2 Fig for experimental data on single and double deflections). Incorporating the single-deflection experiments into the model provides a better prediction of the area in which a single touch can lead to two APs and thus trap closure (cite106†Fig 3A ).\nL282: In the slow-velocity deflection experiments, we were able to determine whether trap triggering happened while advancing (n = 6) or retracting (n = 11) the force probe. Our results indicate that both back and forward displacements contribute to the overall RP level. AP measurements disclosed how these single deflections lead to trap closure.\nL283: When the trap closed during the advancement of the force probe, two APs, one shortly after the other, were observed during the bending of the hair (advancing the probe) (cite111†Fig 4C and cite116†S3 Video ). In the second case (triggering during retraction), one AP was fired during initial bending and a second while retracting the sensor probe as the hair returned to its original position (cite111†Fig 4D ). In both cases, the second AP led to immediate trap closure.\nL284: ### Quantification of the trap snap forces and torques\nL285: In addition to the deflection parameters, we also measured the snap force developed by the triggered trap (cite117†Fig 5A ). A median snap force F_{close} of 73 mN was determined from 48 different traps (cite117†Fig 5B ), ranging from 18 to 174 mN in extreme cases.\nL286: These values are slightly lower than the previously published 140–150 mN measured with a piezoelectric sensor film [cite118†29 , cite119†30 ]; however, we measured the force at the beginning of snapping while Volkov and colleagues measured the impact of the lobe rims.\nL287: Because the measured force strongly depends on the force sensor's position, as well as on the orientation and size of the leaf, the closing torque around the midrib τ_{close} with a median of 0.65 mN m is a better quantity to characterize the trap's closing force (cite117†Fig 5C ).\nL288: The delay time, i.e., the time between the mechanical stimulus and the start of trap closure, was 0.6 ± 0.3 s (mean ± standard deviation, n = 18) based on video analysis, which is in the same range as the 0.4 s previously reported [cite120†31 ].\nL289: Download:\nL290: \nL291:   * cite121†PNG larger image L292:   * cite122†TIFF original image L293: \nL294: Fig 5. Snap force and torque measured with the snap force sensor.\nL295: (A) Typical force curve after trap closure induced by double deflection. (B) Descriptive statistics of the snap force right below the marginal teeth and (C) the closing torque around the midrib of a triggered flytrap. The horizontal line indicates the median, the box the 25th and 75th percentiles, and the whiskers extend to the most extreme data points. For the underlying data, see sheets Fig 5A to Fig 5C in cite66†S1 Data .\nL296: \nL297: cite123†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.g005†doi.org --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDemystifying the Venus flytrap action potential - Hedrich - 2023 - New Phytologist - Wiley Online Library (https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.19113)\nciteturn18view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn15view2\",\"lineno\":115}); Redirected to URL: https://nph.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/nph.19113; Total lines: 450\nL64: Molecular Plant Physiology and Biophysics, Julius-von-Sachs Institute for Biosciences, Biocenter, Würzburg University, Julius-von-Sachs-Platz 2, D-97082 Würzburg, Germany\nL65: \nL66: Author for correspondence:\nL67: \nL68: Rainer Hedrich\nL69: \nL70: Email:cite14†[email protected] L71: \nL72: cite7†Search for more papers by this author L73: \nL74: cite11†Ines Kreuzer ,\nL75: \nL76: Ines Kreuzer\nL77: \nL78:   * cite12†orcid.org/0000-0002-8772-783X†orcid.org L79: Molecular Plant Physiology and Biophysics, Julius-von-Sachs Institute for Biosciences, Biocenter, Würzburg University, Julius-von-Sachs-Platz 2, D-97082 Würzburg, Germany\nL80: \nL81: cite11†Search for more papers by this author L82: \nL83: First published: 10 July 2023\nL84: \nL85: cite15†https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.19113Digital Object Identifier (DOI)†doi.org L86: \nL87: view metrics\nL88: \nL89: Sections\nL90: \nL91: CITE\nL92: \nL93: cite16†Tools L94: \nL95: Share full-text access\nL96: Please review our cite17†Terms and Conditions of Use†onlinelibrary.wiley.com and check box below to share full-text version of article.\nL97: \nL98: [Input]I have read and accept the Wiley Online Library Terms and Conditions of Use\nL99: \nL100: * * *\nL101: \nL102: Shareable Link\nL103: \nL104: Use the link below to share a full-text version of this article with your friends and colleagues. cite18†Learn more.†onlinelibrary.wiley.com L105: \nL106: [Button: Copy URL]\nL107: \nL108: Share a link\nL109: \nL110: Share on\nL111: ## Summary\nL112: All plants are electrically excitable, but only few are known to fire a well-defined, all-or-nothing action potential (AP). The Venus flytrap Dionaea muscipula displays APs with an extraordinarily high firing frequency and speed, enabling the capture organ of this carnivorous plant to catch small animals as fast as flies. The number of APs triggered by the prey is counted and serves as the basis for decisions within the flytrap's hunting cycle.\nL113: The archetypical Dionaea AP lasts 1 s and consists of five phases: Starting from the resting state, an initial cytosolic Ca^{2+} transient is followed by depolarization, repolarization and a transient hyperpolarization (overshoot) before the original membrane potential is finally recovered. When the flytrap matures and becomes excitable, a distinct set of ion channels, pumps and carriers is expressed, each mastering a distinct AP phase.\nL114:  | Contents\nL115:  | cite19†Summary | 2108\nL116: I.  | cite20†Introduction | 2108\nL117: II.  | cite21†Development of trap excitability | 2109\nL118: III.  | cite22†AP is a feature of all fast-moving plants | 2110\nL119: IV.  | cite23†Role of ion channels in electrical signalling and ionic osmolyte flow | 2111\nL120: V.  | cite24†Concluding remarks | 2111\nL121:  | cite25†Acknowledgements | 2111\nL122:  | cite26†References | 2112\nL123: ## I. Introduction\nL124: The Venus flytrap's capability of firing action potentials (APs) is known since Darwin's time from touch-induced membrane potential changes, first monitored by Burdon-Sanderson (cite27†1873 ). Besides Dionaea – according to Charles Darwin – the ‘most wonderful plant of the world’ (Darwin, cite28†1875 ), other actively hunting botanical carnivores also fire APs when in contact with prey (Hedrich & Fukushima, cite29†2021 ).\nL125: The flytrap recognizes collisions with living prey or touch by experimenters with the help of trigger hairs emerging from the inner trap surface. These sensory organs are equally spaced, three on each trap lobe (Fig. cite30†1a ). A 3° displacement of a trigger hair by a force of 30 μN – equivalent to the power of a mosquito – is sufficient to provoke an AP (Scherzer et al., cite31†2019 ). The first AP, elicited by the initial touch deflection of a single trigger hair, is memorized for c. 20 s.\nL126: If the same or another trigger hair is stimulated within these 20 s to bring a second AP on its way, the trap closes within 0.1 s. Remarkably, a calcium wave travels together and with the same fast speed as the AP (Fig. cite30†1b ). Both signals propagate with up to 10 cm s^{−1} along both trap lobes, a velocity unrivalled by other plant systems.\nL127: The cytosolic Ca^{2+} transients of the first and second wave sum up, and upon reaching a critical threshold, they induce the release of viscoelastic energy stored in the open trap, making the trap snap shut (Suda et al., cite32†2020 ). While the entrapped prey tries to escape, it will keep colliding with the trigger hairs and thus sustains AP firing.\nL128: After having counted and integrated ‘2’ in the process of AP-dependent trap snapping, the following three to five or more APs are translated into jasmonate (JA) biosynthesis and signalling (Böhm et al., cite33†2016 ).\nL129: While the fast trap closure does not require JA, the touch hormone stimulates slow hermetical sealing of the capture organ and the activation of glands that cover the inner surface, which leads to the secretion of digestive enzymes into the newly formed green stomach (Scherzer et al., cite34†2017 ).\nL130: Fig. 1\nL131: \nL132: cite16†Open in figure viewer cite35†PowerPoint L133: (a) Open Dionaea muscipula trap. Trigger hairs located at the inner trap surface serve as mechanosensors: their displacement provokes the action potential. (b) Upon touch perception, calcium waves and action potentials (APs) are released and together propagate along both trap lobes.\nL134: (c) Dionaea's AP consists of five distinct phases: after an initial Ca^{2+} influx via DmGLR3.6, the Ca^{2+} signal is amplified by a Ca^{2+}-induced Ca^{2+} release (CICR) from the endoplasmic reticulum to an all-or-nothing cytosolic Ca^{2+} transient (phase 1). If the Ca^{2+} level passes the threshold, Cl^{−} release via Ca^{2+}-dependent ALMT anion channels triggers an initial, rapid depolarization (phase 2).\nL135: Positive of −60 mV, depolarization-dependent DmSKOR K^{+} channels and DmAHA4 H^{+} pumps are activated and slow the depolarization kinetics (1^{st} shoulder). At c. −20 mV, when anion efflux and K^{+} efflux compensate each other, the depolarization maximum is reached. The repolarization (phase 3) sets in when [Ca^{2+}]_{cytosolic} drops, the anion channel inactivates, and DmSKOR and DmAHA4 allow K^{+} and H^{+} release from the cell.\nL136: Negative of −60 mV DmSKOR inactivates, slowing the fast, initial repolarization (2^{nd} shoulder). The delayed deactivation of the depolarization-activated H^{+} pump results in sustained H^{+} export, thus provoking a transient overshoot hyperpolarization (phase 4). During membrane hyperpolarization, DmAHA4 voltage inactivates and H^{+}-driven DmHAK5 and NRT-type anion transporters reset the pre-AP resting potential by recharging the K^{+} and anion battery (phase 5).\nL137: (d) Two APs drive the cytosolic Ca^{2+} level above the threshold upon which rapid trap closure is unavoidable. Snap buckling is achieved by the release of K^{+} and anions and, in turn, turgor loss in the motor cells of the inner leaflet. As a result, the metastable hydrostatic pressure balance between inner and outer leaflet collapses and the open concave trap geometry flips over to the convex closed one.\nL138: The Venus flytrap Dyscalculia (DYSC) mutant cannot properly count and decode the Ca^{2+}-APs and is thus unable to snap close its trap and to produce sufficient JA in response to prolonged mechanostimulation. The JA-dependent steps of the hunting cycle can, however, be rescued by external JA application in DYSC (Iosip et al., cite36†2023 ). This shows that prey capture, recognition and processing are under the control of the Ca^{2+} and the JA clock.\n"
